WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.020
Despite being the kind of nailbiter we've&nbsp;
come to expect from The Walking Dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.680
the series finale didn't go down well&nbsp;
with everyone. Here are the moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.477
from the final episode that upset people the most.

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:14.640
Near the end of the Commonwealth storyline,&nbsp;
Daryl does something that fans of TV shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.260
either love or hate — he says the name&nbsp;
of the show. In a brief, dramatic – well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.180
as dramatic as Daryl ever gets – speech to Pamela&nbsp;
and the Commonwealth soldiers, he gruffly says,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.920
"We ain't the walking dead."

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.800
Then he boss-walks toward the&nbsp;
gate without another thought.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.080
It's a cool moment, and a lot of fans ate it up.&nbsp;
Twitter ran rampant with reactions to the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.860
a lot of which were positive. However,&nbsp;
there were plenty of tweets making fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.520
of the title drop, and some people&nbsp;
flat out hated it. One user tweeted,

00:00:48.095 --> 00:00:51.155
It's a clear callback to Rick Grimes in Season 5:

00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:53.161
"We are the walking dead."

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.580
Easter egg or not, Norman&nbsp;
Reedus revealed to Vanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.040
Fair that he had to be talked into it. He said:

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.220
After 12 years of The Walking Dead killing off&nbsp;
main characters and practically being driven by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.020
death, it's widely accepted that no one is safe.&nbsp;
We've seen beloved characters take their leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.320
including a number of children.&nbsp;
One issue with the finale is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.100
spin-offs have already been announced, so&nbsp;
a number of the survivors — like Daryl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.040
Maggie, and Negan — were at no&nbsp;
real risk of being killed off.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.200
While all fans have characters they root for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.080
most were expecting to see a&nbsp;
bit more bloodshed. On Reddit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.420
the general consensus seems to be that the finale&nbsp;
lacked any meaningful deaths. One user wrote:

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.540
Another bone fans have to pick with the&nbsp;
finale was how it handled the lost members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.620
of Magna's group. The writers brought Luke&nbsp;
and Jules back seemingly out of nowhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.100
just to abruptly kill Jules and&nbsp;
kick off the finale by killing Luke.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.780
There's no denying that the heavily emotional&nbsp;
death of Luke, still reeling over the loss of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.100
girlfriend and in pain from the loss of his leg,&nbsp;
started the episode out with a bang that seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.020
to promise a finale that takes no prisoners. Luke&nbsp;
would turn out to be one of the only survivors to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.040
die, though, which makes his sudden return&nbsp;
and abrupt exit feel somewhat sacrificial.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.000
Many fans saw Luke's death as nothing&nbsp;
more than a way to squeeze some gore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.940
in without killing off a main&nbsp;
character. One commenter wrote:

00:02:16.638 --> 00:02:17.838
While another said that,

00:02:21.563 --> 00:02:26.820
The series finale is full of callbacks, including&nbsp;
the way Judith wakes up in a hospital bed. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.840
is how the series began, with her father waking&nbsp;
up in a deserted hospital back in the first-ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.040
episode. While the nostalgic parallel was&nbsp;
nice, many fans think that since the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.000
opened with Rick, it should have closed&nbsp;
on him, too. It's true that a lot of fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.720
on Reddit have said that Rick was present&nbsp;
in more of the finale than they expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.320
which is a great thing. However, there are&nbsp;
those who wanted it handled differently.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.400
One user wrote:

00:03:04.314 --> 00:03:07.740
Others thought the Rick scenes were a&nbsp;
letdown. According to a second user,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:17.340
Fans missed Michonne after Danai Gurira exited&nbsp;
The Walking Dead following Season 10. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.300
people wanted and expected her to pop up in&nbsp;
the finale to help her fellow survivors out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:25.380
of another tight spot. Michonne has always had&nbsp;
a way of coming out of nowhere to save the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.560
just like she did with the formula for&nbsp;
Judith all the way back at the prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.340
So it made sense that she would return&nbsp;
to play some part in the curtain-closer.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.260
"And if it's me who doesn't make it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.740
you're going to have to lead the others&nbsp;
forward. Because you're the one who can."

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.480
Her presence in the finale isn't what upset fans&nbsp;
— people didn't like the way she was dressed.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.400
Michonne has always looked great no matter who&nbsp;
she's fighting or how covered in blood she gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.080
but her appearance in the Walking Dead finale had&nbsp;
fans scratching their heads. Michonne is spotless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.920
and wearing intricate fighting leathers,&nbsp;
which led fans to wonder where the heck she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.960
got her clothes. Many noted that Danai Gurira&nbsp;
looked more like Okoye, her Marvel character.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.280
One fan commented,

00:04:11.437 --> 00:04:14.917
As the only survivor from the main group&nbsp;
who's been around for a long while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.917 --> 00:04:19.440
Rosita's death was hard to take. Unlike many&nbsp;
of the other main character deaths we've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.040
in the series, hers wasn't bloody. She&nbsp;
gets bitten after falling into a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.060
of walkers with the newly recovered baby&nbsp;
Coco strapped to her chest. After a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.440
of badass maneuvers get the child to safety,&nbsp;
Rosita reveals she's been bitten on the back.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.540
It's typical for The Walking Dead to handle a main&nbsp;
character death with a short-lived high marred by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.280
tragedy, but fans just aren't buying the sequence&nbsp;
of events leading to Rosita's death. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.740
Digital Spy called Rosita's end the dumbest death&nbsp;
in the entire series. A lot of fans agreed, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:51.480
can't really blame them, because not much about it&nbsp;
makes sense. For one thing, the placement of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.040
bite seems off, considering that she's wearing&nbsp;
numerous layers of clothing. There's also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.300
fact that she's unable to make a jump that Eugene&nbsp;
made before her, even though she's leagues ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.980
of him in physical ability. And, as one Redditor&nbsp;
pointed out, her actual passing was unconvincing.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.100
The three previously announced Walking&nbsp;
Dead spin-offs, while good news for fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.900
proved problematic for the finale of the original&nbsp;
series. First, knowing that most fan-favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.760
characters couldn't be killed off drastically&nbsp;
lowered the stakes. To make matters worse, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.260
pending spin-offs influenced the finale in terms&nbsp;
of the story. For some fans, the send-off episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.880
was too much like a backdoor pilot for future&nbsp;
shows, rather than giving a solid, satisfying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.200
ending to the original series. Fans were quick&nbsp;
to call the show out for this over on Reddit.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.700
One user writes,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.320
While it certainly makes sense to touch on where&nbsp;
the fan-favorite characters have been and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.835
heading in the finale, squeezing in intros&nbsp;
to three separate spin-offs is a bit much.

00:05:56.835 --> 00:05:59.512
"We are endless."

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:04.260
Partially because of how much focus the finale&nbsp;
gave the upcoming spin-offs, the last episode of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.700
the long-running series wasn't a true finale&nbsp;
at all. Even though the series was ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.900
it was really just a new beginning. It's almost&nbsp;
as if production was afraid to give fans true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.320
closure, in case they wouldn't want to&nbsp;
continue watching the future spin-offs.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.800
Forbes senior contributor and&nbsp;
author Paul Tassi called a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.560
of the creative decisions and some&nbsp;
of the more emotional scenes "cheap."

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:32.374
"I think of the dead all the time.&nbsp;
And about the living, who I lost."

00:06:32.374 --> 00:06:36.360
According to Tassi, these scenes were there&nbsp;
to give the episode more of a finale feel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.576
but in truth, the episode lacked&nbsp;
any real shocks or twists.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.475
"We have a lot to be proud of. Our&nbsp;
family. Our community. The future."

00:06:46.475 --> 00:06:49.080
Tassi definitely isn't alone in this opinion.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.860
To call something that spends half its&nbsp;
time talking up future storylines a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.060
finale has been labeled a cop-out by a&nbsp;
lot of fans. There's an ongoing joke on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.720
Reddit about how the series will never&nbsp;
really end. In the words of one user,

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:07.800
"You'll keep making it better."

00:07:10.135 --> 00:07:11.276
"You will, too."

00:07:11.276 --> 00:07:12.660
While another user added,

00:07:16.153 --> 00:07:20.220
Another way the impending spin-offs affected&nbsp;
the finale is that the inclusion of so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:24.120
set-up made the rest of the episode feel&nbsp;
rushed. This isn't just a finale problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.620
though. The entire Commonwealth plot that ended&nbsp;
the show wasn't as well put together as it should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.580
have been. According to CBR, production was&nbsp;
forced to rush the Commonwealth plotline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:36.600
despite it being a huge undertaking. The&nbsp;
speedy run through the storyline did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.554
finale no favors. As Collider put it, the&nbsp;
episode felt "rushed" and "chaotic" at times.

00:07:41.554 --> 00:07:50.314
"You disappeared hundreds of citizens. Led&nbsp;
the dead to our doorstep. You shot a child."

00:07:50.314 --> 00:07:52.860
With so many survivors and&nbsp;
storylines coming together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.220
ticking every box was always&nbsp;
going to be tricky. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:00.780
the finale contained too many leaps of logic.&nbsp;
An example of this is trying to nullify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.689
threat of zombies by blowing up a portion of the&nbsp;
Commonwealth. How is this a quick fix solution?

00:08:05.689 --> 00:08:09.780
One thing that never seemed right about the&nbsp;
Commonwealth storyline is that we never really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.880
see the place. The Commonwealth is supposed&nbsp;
to be made up of around 50,000 survivors. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.260
we never see just how expansive and diverse&nbsp;
the community really is. The only plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.160
explanation was that the creators were waiting&nbsp;
for the finale to unleash the full scale of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:27.600
the Commonwealth and its population, which&nbsp;
never happened, leaving fans underwhelmed.

00:08:28.032 --> 00:08:29.298
One Redditor writes,

00:08:34.895 --> 00:08:36.720
Another user then claimed that the lack of numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.500
in these big scenes was a hangover&nbsp;
from COVID-19 restrictions, writing,

00:08:48.178 --> 00:08:52.080
Considering how much The Walking Dead&nbsp;
focuses on the themes of family and loyalty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.380
the survivors sure do leave their&nbsp;
kids behind a lot. It obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.100
takes a village to raise a child in a&nbsp;
zombie apocalypse, we get that. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.400
all the child abandonment becomes a&nbsp;
bit too much when parents go off on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:06.155
their own missions, leaving their young&nbsp;
ones behind for the foreseeable future.

00:09:06.391 --> 00:09:07.079
"Daryl?"

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:08.280
"Yeah."

00:09:09.814 --> 00:09:11.614
"You deserve a happy ending, too."

00:09:11.614 --> 00:09:15.000
The Grimes children don't see a&nbsp;
lot of their parents. While RJ and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.820
Judith are lucky to have seasoned&nbsp;
survivors as parental figures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.957
it doesn't matter much when they're not&nbsp;
around. As one concerned Redditor put it:

00:09:26.553 --> 00:09:27.998
Another user doubled down with,

00:09:35.215 --> 00:09:39.000
For many fans on Reddit, the biggest problem&nbsp;
with the finale is a lack of closure.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.320
As one commenter put it,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.165
While another user added,

00:09:48.896 --> 00:09:51.060
And a third piled on with an excellent point:

00:10:00.894 --> 00:10:04.860
"We're together. Pieces of a whole that just keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.840
going for what we gave each&nbsp;
other. One unstoppable life."

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.640
While recognizing the finale's good moments,&nbsp;
Vulture's episode recap focuses on the lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:18.960
of solid closure, and comments on the oddity&nbsp;
of having the finale end with the promise of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.493
bright future while also preparing us to see the&nbsp;
spin-off survivors living through more struggles.

00:10:23.493 --> 00:10:26.100
"I want to talk about the future. There's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:29.553
out there to find out about.&nbsp;
And I think it's time we did."

00:10:29.553 --> 00:10:32.760
It's like the finale feigns closure,&nbsp;
but there's actually none given.

